>> source_note: Quantities, timing, and operational sequence are transcribed from the cited public recipe page. Read the source page for its full visual guidance and context.

## Ingredients

### Main

@flank steak{10%oz}
@yellow onion{0.5}
@bean sprouts{1%handful}
@scallions{3%stalks}
@fresh flat rice noodles{1%lb}

### Beef marinade

@baking soda{1%tsp}
@cornstarch{1%tbsp}
@oyster sauce{1%tbsp}
@soy sauce{1%tbsp}
@tamari or dark soy sauce{1%tsp}
@white pepper{0.25%tsp}
@water{2%tbsp}
@cooking wine{1%tsp}
@sugar{1%tsp}
@cooking oil{1%tbsp}

### Chow fun sauce

@tamari or dark soy sauce{1%tsp}
@soy sauce{1%tbsp}
@oyster sauce{1%tbsp}

### For stir-frying

@cooking oil{3%tbsp}
@sesame oil{1%tsp}

## Method

1. Slice @flank steak{10%oz} into medium or 1/4-inch slices. Rinse in room-temperature water, then drain and squeeze out excess water.
2. Slice @yellow onion{0.5} to 1/4-inch thickness. Wash the @bean sprouts{1%handful}. Cut @scallions{3%stalks} into 1-inch pieces and separate white from green parts.
3. Mix the marinade ingredients except @cooking oil{1%tbsp}. Coat the beef, then mix in @cooking oil{1%tbsp}.
4. Loosen @fresh flat rice noodles{1%lb} completely before heating the wok. Mix the chow fun sauce in a small bowl.
5. Heat the wok on the highest heat for ~{1.5%minutes}. Add @cooking oil{3%tbsp}; heat about ~{30%seconds} until it ripples. Stir-fry the beef for 40 to 60 seconds; remove it while still slightly red.
6. Wipe down the wok. Fry onion and scallion whites for 20 to 30 seconds until fragrant. Add noodles and disturb them as little as possible.
7. Cook noodles for ~{2%minutes} until golden and aromatic. Add the prepared sauce and cook ~{1%minute} until coated.
8. Add bean sprouts and scallion greens; cook for 30 to 45 seconds. Return the beef, cook ~{1%minute}, turn off the heat, and add @sesame oil{1%tsp}.

## Source checkpoints

- The source calls for the wok to be fully hot before oil and for the oil to ripple before beef is added.
- Do not over-stir fresh rice noodles; this prevents breakage.
- The finished dish is dry rather than saucy, with no pool of sauce at the plate bottom.
